Z||000000||Bluebottlejunky||10-14-2001||11:34 PM||||About what time do you guys put your Fieros away for the Winter?  I just found a winter beater that I think will be good, so I can put mine away!  I have some really good plans Change oil pan/take of body panels to prep for painting/change some interior parts etc.  O the fun this winter should be!<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: <B>KilleRedGT</B>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Tigger||10-14-2001||11:54 PM||||Whadda talking about, there's plenty of good driving time left, at least a good month or so.<P>I'll be driving mine until there's snow on the roads or, well, when my Fiero looks like a ground mole going through the snow.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bluebottlejunky||10-14-2001||11:56 PM||||Ahhhh, yes I know I know! but im excited to learn alot this winter..||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||10-14-2001||11:59 PM||||Best thing not to do is lay if up for the whole winter. Mine gets started once a week and I try to get it out once a month for a trip around the block if there's no snow/salt on the road. This just saves a lot of work doing a full preservation for winter storage. Really feel it's better for the car also.<P>If you do lay it up, there's a lot of items to consider. There was a string on this about a week back that had information.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bluebottlejunky||10-15-2001||12:06 AM||||yup, I will start her up and give he a good drive..when its nice..so it dosen't dry up like a prun!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||10-15-2001||12:10 AM||||hehe yep.  I do the same.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-15-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000006||RiceCooker||10-15-2001||12:38 PM||||I have a week and a half left before she goes away<P>199,300 on the odometer (tryin to get 200)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||10-15-2001||01:05 PM||||I haven't really put it up the last couple winters like I was doing.  When I initially got the car, I would do all kinds of prep stuff to it and totally take it off the road.  Now it just sits under a cover between days when I can take it for a spin.  The only months that I have a real problem with are December, January and maybe the first half of February.  Otherwise there are enough nice days mixed in to get it out occasionally.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||MinnGreenGT||10-15-2001||04:52 PM||||I'm not speaking for others here in the Arctic State (It's been proven that the average winter temp in Minnesota is lower than that of Anchorage Alaska...) but I'm not through with the season yet!  I still need to check around to see if I can afford a local winter storage- where I can get to the car regularly.  For a number of years I've been storing my GT in a farmer-friend's barn.  Nice size, cheap ($75 season), and farily safe (inconspicuous)- but low on the Property.  When spring comes, I usually get stuck waiting a couple of extra weeks for the ground to dry out- or jump the season early and hope the ground is frozen enough to drive on (or end up stuck in the mud).<P>I don't do anything too specific for storage... just a full tank of gas, everything cleaned recently, tape off the vent openings (mice love to get in 'em), and a car cover.  The other reason I want to go to a local storage place, is the reduction of mice over a farm setting.  This past winter, they bedded down in my trunk and basically ruined my carpet!<P>(praying for a late 1st snow...)<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||GTFiero1||10-15-2001||05:50 PM||||if any of yous are planning long term storage this is what you should do. put you car up on jack stands to get the weight off the tires, take off air filter and cover the opening with a plastic back, if your storing it close to a year or more you would want to drain all fluids (but remember to put them back in when you finally start up the car again), put a plate with some baking soda on the floor so it will soak up any moister and lastly buy a good breathable car cover.<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
